Step 4: Generator (generator.sv)

The generator creates random serial input bits and sends them to the driver through a mailbox.

class generator;

  mailbox gen2drv;

  function new(mailbox gen2drv);
    this.gen2drv = gen2drv;
  endfunction

  task run();

    transaction tr;

    repeat(20)
    begin
      tr = new();

      assert(tr.randomize());

      $display("\nGenerator Generated Bit = %0b", tr.serial_in);

      gen2drv.put(tr);

      #10;
    end

  endtask

endclass
Purpose
Generates random 0s and 1s.
Creates 20 transactions.
Sends each transaction to the driver using a mailbox.
Step 5: Driver (driver.sv)

The driver receives transactions from the generator and applies them to the DUT.

class driver;

  virtual siso_if vif;
  mailbox gen2drv;

  function new(mailbox gen2drv,
               virtual siso_if vif);

    this.gen2drv = gen2drv;
    this.vif = vif;

  endfunction


  task run();

    transaction tr;

    forever
    begin

      gen2drv.get(tr);

      @(posedge vif.clk);

      vif.serial_in <= tr.serial_in;

      $display("Driver Applied Bit = %0b",
               tr.serial_in);

    end

  endtask

endclass
Purpose
Receives one transaction at a time.
Waits for the positive clock edge.
Drives the serial input bit to the DUT.
